  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The residual scale is underspecified: raw item embeddings and normalized SASRec states may have different norms. Adding alpha*r changes score magnitudes and gradient paths, so improvements cannot automatically be attributed to better recent-interest modeling.",
    "Weights proportional to exp(-j) assign about 64% of a five-item pool to the latest item. A last-item-only residual control is needed to distinguish recent-history modeling from a simple item-transition shortcut.",
    "The hypothesis lacks a predefined diagnostic statistic, decision threshold, and predicted intervention outcome. One successful ablation would demonstrate improvement, but provide limited evidence that the diagnostic predicts improvement.",
    "Reproduction requires explicit pooling rules for padding, repeated items, current-position inclusion, embedding scaling and dropout, plus a checkpoint policy and a trial allocation within the shared GPU budget.",
    "Originality 4 is plausible, but future potential 8 remains speculative. Difficulty 4 may underestimate controlled probes, predictor parity, and state restoration. The residual also cannot directly resolve unsupported compatibility or functional-complementarity assumptions."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Clarify residual normalization and causal implementation so observed gains can be separated from score-scale and optimization effects.",
      "query": "In SASRec implementations, how do item-embedding scaling, final LayerNorm, embedding dropout, and tied dot-product scoring affect adding a causal recent-item embedding residual q_t = h_t + alpha*r_t? What controls distinguish recency gains from representation-norm or logit-scale changes?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Assess novelty and identify a minimal control that distinguishes a five-item residual from an established last-item transition mechanism.",
      "query": "How do FPMC, FOSSIL, and recent short-term/long-term sequential recommenders combine last-item transitions with pooled history? For a SASRec residual using five items weighted by exp(-age), which ablations distinguish last-item similarity, uniform pooling, and order-sensitive recent-history information?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Make the diagnostic-to-intervention claim falsifiable while accounting for repeated validation selection and the limited search budget.",
      "query": "What experimental methods validate whether training-history perturbation diagnostics predict recommendation-model improvements? Consider prespecified directional predictions, matched perturbation controls, paired user-level HR@10 uncertainty, and selection bias when comparing three models on fixed validation users with 99 sampled negatives."
    }
  ]
}
